---
// start-running.astro — Beginner running guide page
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CTASection from '../components/CTASection.astro';
import { url } from '../utils/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Start Running — Beginner's Complete Guide"
  description="New to running? Our beginner's guide covers everything: how to prepare, warm up, pace yourself, avoid common mistakes and follow a 4-week training plan to build your running habit."
>
  <!-- Page Hero -->
  <section class="page-hero bg-dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="page-hero__content">
        <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="breadcrumb breadcrumb--light">
          <ol role="list">
            <li><a href={url('')}>Home</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li aria-current="page">Start Running</li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
        <span class="page-hero__eyebrow">Your First Steps</span>
        <h1 class="page-hero__title text-white">How to Start Running<br/>as a Complete Beginner</h1>
        <p class="page-hero__subtitle">
          Everything you need to begin — from lacing up for the first time
          to completing your first continuous run.
        </p>
        <div class="page-hero__meta">
          <span>📖 Approx. 15 min read</span>
          <span>·</span>
          <span>✅ Includes 4-week plan</span>
          <span>·</span>
          <span>⚕️ Includes health disclaimer</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Table of Contents -->
  <section class="toc-section bg-ivory">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="Page contents">
        <h2 class="toc__heading">In This Guide</h2>
        <ol class="toc__list">
          <li><a href="#getting-started">Getting Started — What You Actually Need</a></li>
          <li><a href="#first-run">Preparing for Your First Run</a></li>
          <li><a href="#warmup">Warm-Up and Cool-Down Routines</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pacing">Pacing — The Beginner's Golden Rule</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes to Avoid</a></li>
          <li><a href="#four-week-plan">4-Week Beginner Training Plan</a></li>
          <li><a href="#motivation">Staying Consistent and Motivated</a></li>
          <li><a href="#disclaimer">Health and Safety Disclaimer</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Main content -->
  <article class="guide-content">
    <div class="container container--narrow">

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section id="getting-started" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Getting Started — What You Actually Need</h2>
        <p>
          One of the most common barriers to starting running is the belief that you
          need expensive gear, a gym membership or a certain level of fitness before you begin.
          That is not true. The barrier to entry for running is deliberately low.
        </p>
        <p>
          Here is what you genuinely need before your first run:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A supportive pair of running shoes.</strong> You do not need the most expensive model, but footwear that fits well and provides appropriate cushioning reduces the risk of discomfort and injury. See our <a href={url('running-gear/')}>Running Gear Guide</a> for practical advice.</li>
          <li><strong>Comfortable clothing.</strong> Breathable, moisture-wicking fabric helps regulate temperature. Avoid heavy cotton on warm days.</li>
          <li><strong>A safe, flat route.</strong> A local park, quiet road or running track is ideal for beginners. Flat ground is easier on the joints when you are starting out.</li>
          <li><strong>Water.</strong> Stay hydrated before and after your run. For runs under 30 minutes, you likely do not need to carry water during the run itself.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="info-box">
          <strong>💡 Start Simple</strong><br/>
          Many runners begin with nothing more than decent shoes, comfortable clothing and a willingness to step outside. Start with what you have.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <section id="first-run" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Preparing for Your First Run</h2>
        <p>
          Your first run does not have to be long, fast or impressive. The single objective
          on day one is to complete a short session and feel capable of going again.
        </p>
        <h3>Before You Head Out</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Eat a light meal or snack 1–2 hours before. Avoid running on a completely empty stomach or immediately after a heavy meal.</li>
          <li>Drink water in the hour before your run.</li>
          <li>Choose a time that fits your schedule consistently — morning, lunchtime or evening all work depending on your preference.</li>
          <li>Tell someone where you are going, particularly for evening runs.</li>
          <li>Take your phone if you are running in an unfamiliar area.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>What to Expect</h3>
        <p>
          It is normal to feel uncomfortable during your first few runs. Your breathing may
          feel laboured, your legs may feel heavy and you may need to walk. This is not failure —
          it is adaptation. Your cardiovascular system and muscles are adjusting to an unfamiliar demand.
          Within two to three weeks, the same effort begins to feel noticeably easier.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="warmup" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Warm-Up and Cool-Down Routines</h2>
        <h3>Why Warming Up Matters</h3>
        <p>
          A short warm-up increases blood flow to your muscles, raises your heart rate gradually
          and prepares your joints for the range of motion required during running.
          It does not need to be elaborate.
        </p>
        <h3>Simple 5-Minute Warm-Up</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>2 minutes of brisk walking or marching in place</li>
          <li>10 leg swings on each side (front-to-back and side-to-side)</li>
          <li>10 hip circles on each side</li>
          <li>10 walking lunges</li>
          <li>30 seconds of light jogging in place</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Cooling Down After Your Run</h3>
        <p>
          A cool-down helps your heart rate return to resting levels gradually and supports recovery.
          Walk for 2–3 minutes after your run, then perform gentle stretches targeting the calves,
          quadriceps, hamstrings and hip flexors. Hold each stretch for 20–30 seconds.
        </p>
        <div class="info-box">
          <strong>⚠️ Note on Stretching</strong><br/>
          Current sports science evidence does not strongly support static stretching immediately
          before a run. Save deep stretching for after you have finished.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="pacing" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Pacing — The Beginner's Golden Rule</h2>
        <p>
          Beginners most commonly make the mistake of running too fast. This leads to rapid
          breathlessness, early fatigue and a discouraging experience.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use the <strong>conversational pace test</strong>: if you can hold a conversation
          without gasping between words, your pace is appropriate for easy running.
          If you cannot string a sentence together, you are running too hard.
        </p>
        <h3>What Easy Running Feels Like</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Your breathing is elevated but controlled.</li>
          <li>You feel like you could continue for significantly longer.</li>
          <li>You are working but not struggling.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For most beginners, this means running much more slowly than feels natural.
          That is correct. Speed develops naturally over time as your fitness builds.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="mistakes" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
        <div class="mistakes-grid">
          {[
            {
              mistake: 'Running too fast too soon',
              fix: 'Use conversational pace. If you cannot speak in short sentences, slow down.',
            },
            {
              mistake: 'Skipping the warm-up',
              fix: 'Even a 3-minute walk before running reduces injury risk and improves performance.',
            },
            {
              mistake: 'Increasing mileage too quickly',
              fix: 'A common guideline is not to increase weekly running distance by more than 10% per week.',
            },
            {
              mistake: 'Ignoring early pain signals',
              fix: 'Discomfort from effort is normal. Sharp or localised joint pain is a signal to stop and rest.',
            },
            {
              mistake: 'Running every day as a beginner',
              fix: 'Two to three sessions per week with rest days allows your body to recover and adapt.',
            },
            {
              mistake: 'Wearing the wrong footwear',
              fix: 'Running in flat, unsupportive or worn-out shoes increases injury risk. See our gear guide.',
            },
          ].map(item => (
            <div class="mistake-card">
              <div class="mistake-card__header">
                <span aria-hidden="true">❌</span>
                <h4>{item.mistake}</h4>
              </div>
              <p class="mistake-card__fix"><strong>What to do instead:</strong> {item.fix}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-xl)">
          For a deeper dive, read our article:
          <a href={url('blog/common-running-mistakes-beginners-should-avoid/')}>
            Common Running Mistakes Beginners Should Avoid
          </a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6: 4-week plan -->
      <section id="four-week-plan" class="guide-section">
        <h2>4-Week Beginner Training Plan</h2>
        <div class="info-box">
          <strong>⚕️ Important:</strong> This is a general educational training framework.
          It is not personalised medical or fitness advice. Consult a healthcare professional
          or qualified fitness instructor before beginning a new exercise programme,
          particularly if you have an existing health condition, injury or have been
          sedentary for an extended period.
        </div>
        <p>
          This plan uses an interval structure — alternating walking and running — to build
          your cardiovascular fitness and running-specific strength progressively.
          Each session should take between 20 and 30 minutes including warm-up and cool-down.
        </p>
        <p>
          Complete <strong>three sessions per week</strong> with at least one rest day between sessions.
        </p>

        <div class="plan-weeks">
          {[
            {
              week: 'Week 1',
              theme: 'Building the Habit',
              colour: '#FF6B35',
              sessions: [
                { day: 'Session 1', detail: '5 min warm-up walk → repeat 5× [run 1 min / walk 4 min] → 3 min cool-down walk' },
                { day: 'Session 2', detail: '5 min warm-up walk → repeat 5× [run 1 min / walk 4 min] → 3 min cool-down walk' },
                { day: 'Session 3', detail: '5 min warm-up walk → repeat 6× [run 1 min / walk 4 min] → 3 min cool-down walk' },
              ],
              tip: 'Focus on completing the session, not speed. Slow down if necessary.',
            },
            {
              week: 'Week 2',
              theme: 'Building Confidence',
              colour: '#e05520',
              sessions: [
                { day: 'Session 1', detail: '5 min warm-up → repeat 5× [run 2 min / walk 3 min] → cool-down' },
                { day: 'Session 2', detail: '5 min warm-up → repeat 5× [run 2 min / walk 3 min] → cool-down' },
                { day: 'Session 3', detail: '5 min warm-up → repeat 5× [run 2.5 min / walk 2.5 min] → cool-down' },
              ],
              tip: 'You may feel more comfortable this week. Keep your pace conversational.',
            },
            {
              week: 'Week 3',
              theme: 'Extending the Runs',
              colour: '#d44a18',
              sessions: [
                { day: 'Session 1', detail: '5 min warm-up → repeat 4× [run 3 min / walk 2 min] → cool-down' },
                { day: 'Session 2', detail: '5 min warm-up → repeat 4× [run 4 min / walk 1 min] → cool-down' },
                { day: 'Session 3', detail: '5 min warm-up → repeat 3× [run 5 min / walk 2 min] → cool-down' },
              ],
              tip: 'Some fatigue is normal. If you need an extra rest day, take it.',
            },
            {
              week: 'Week 4',
              theme: 'First Continuous Run',
              colour: '#c03e10',
              sessions: [
                { day: 'Session 1', detail: '5 min warm-up → run 8 min / walk 2 min / run 8 min → cool-down' },
                { day: 'Session 2', detail: '5 min warm-up → run 12 min / walk 2 min / run 8 min → cool-down' },
                { day: 'Session 3', detail: '5 min warm-up → run 20–25 min continuously → cool-down' },
              ],
              tip: 'Session 3 is your milestone run. Go slowly. You have earned it.',
            },
          ].map(w => (
            <div class="plan-week">
              <div class="plan-week__header" style={`border-left-color:${w.colour}`}>
                <div>
                  <span class="plan-week__number">{w.week}</span>
                  <span class="plan-week__theme">{w.theme}</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="plan-week__sessions">
                {w.sessions.map(s => (
                  <div class="plan-session">
                    <span class="plan-session__label">{s.day}</span>
                    <span class="plan-session__detail">{s.detail}</span>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
              <p class="plan-week__tip">💡 {w.tip}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h3>After Week 4</h3>
        <p>
          Completing this plan means you can run continuously for 20+ minutes — a major achievement
          for a beginner. From here you can explore:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Building toward a Parkrun (5K timed event, free, community-based)</li>
          <li>Increasing your running duration gradually week by week</li>
          <li>Adding a fourth weekly session once you feel consistently comfortable</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 7 -->
      <section id="motivation" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Staying Consistent and Motivated</h2>
        <p>
          Motivation will fluctuate. Building a running habit depends on consistency more
          than any single outstanding session. Here are practical strategies that support
          long-term adherence:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Schedule your runs like appointments.</strong> Block the time in your calendar. Unscheduled workouts are easy to skip.</li>
          <li><strong>Track your progress.</strong> Note your sessions in a running diary or app. Seeing what you have completed is motivating in itself.</li>
          <li><strong>Find a running partner.</strong> Social accountability significantly increases adherence, particularly in the early weeks.</li>
          <li><strong>Prepare the night before.</strong> Lay out your kit before bed. Reducing morning friction improves follow-through.</li>
          <li><strong>Celebrate small wins.</strong> Completing Week 1 is worth acknowledging. Every session you finish is progress.</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid all-or-nothing thinking.</strong> A 15-minute run when you planned 30 is still a run. Partial completion beats skipping.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For more motivational strategies, read:
          <a href={url('blog/how-to-stay-motivated-and-build-a-running-habit/')}>
            How to Stay Motivated and Build a Running Habit
          </a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 8: Disclaimer -->
      <section id="disclaimer" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Health and Safety Disclaimer</h2>
        <div class="disclaimer-box">
          <p>
            The information on this page is provided for general educational purposes only.
            It is not a substitute for personalised medical advice, diagnosis or treatment.
            Always seek the advice of a qualified healthcare professional before beginning
            a new physical exercise programme.
          </p>
          <p>
            This guidance is particularly important if you:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Have a pre-existing medical condition (including cardiovascular or respiratory conditions)</li>
            <li>Are pregnant or recently postpartum</li>
            <li>Are recovering from illness or injury</li>
            <li>Have been sedentary for a prolonged period</li>
            <li>Experience chest pain, dizziness or shortness of breath during exercise</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Stop exercising and seek medical attention if you experience pain, dizziness,
            chest tightness or any unusual symptoms during or after running.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0">
            Start Strong does not accept liability for injury arising from use of the
            training plans or guidance on this website.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Internal links -->
      <section class="guide-section related-content">
        <h2>Continue Your Journey</h2>
        <div class="grid-3">
          {[
            { href: url('blog/how-to-start-running-as-a-complete-beginner/'), label: 'Beginner Running Article' },
            { href: url('running-gear/'), label: 'Running Gear Guide' },
            { href: url('blog/how-to-stay-motivated-and-build-a-running-habit/'), label: 'Motivation & Habits' },
          ].map(link => (
            <a href={link.href} class="related-link-card">
              <span>{link.label}</span>
              <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>

  <CTASection
    heading="You've Got Everything You Need"
    subtext="The plan is ready. The first step is yours. Let's go."
    primaryLabel="Read Our Beginner Article"
    primaryHref="blog/how-to-start-running-as-a-complete-beginner/"
    secondaryLabel="Explore Running Gear"
    secondaryHref="running-gear/"
    gaLabel="start_running_bottom"
  />

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Page hero */
  .page-hero {
    padding-block: var(--space-4xl);
  }

  .page-hero__content { max-width: 700px; }

  .breadcrumb { margin-bottom: var(--space-lg); }
  .breadcrumb ol {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .breadcrumb--light a { color: rgba(255,255,255,0.6); text-decoration: none; }
  .breadcrumb--light a:hover { color: var(--color-orange); }
  .breadcrumb--light li:last-child { color: rgba(255,255,255,0.4); }

  .page-hero__eyebrow {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--color-orange);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .page-hero__title {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.25rem);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .page-hero__subtitle {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7);
    max-width: 560px;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .page-hero__meta {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.5);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  /* TOC */
  .toc-section { padding-block: var(--space-xl); }

  .toc {
    background: var(--color-white);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-grey-light);
    border-radius: var(--border-radius-lg);
    padding: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .toc__heading {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--color-grey);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .toc__list {
    padding-left: var(--space-lg);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .toc__list li { margin-bottom: var(--space-xs); }
  .toc__list a {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--color-grey-dark);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .toc__list a:hover { color: var(--color-orange); }

  /* Guide content */
  .guide-content { padding-block: var(--space-3xl); }

  .guide-section { margin-bottom: var(--space-4xl); }
  .guide-section h2 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-grey-light);
  }
  .guide-section h3 { margin-top: var(--space-xl); margin-bottom: var(--space-sm); }
  .guide-section p  { color: var(--color-grey-dark); line-height: 1.75; }
  .guide-section ul, .guide-section ol { color: var(--color-grey-dark); line-height: 1.75; }

  /* Info box */
  .info-box {
    background-color: var(--color-ivory);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-grey-light);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-orange);
    border-radius: var(--border-radius);
    padding: var(--space-lg);
    margin-block: var(--space-xl);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-grey-dark);
  }

  /* Mistakes grid */
  .mistakes-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .mistake-card {
    background: var(--color-ivory);
    border-radius: var(--border-radius);
    padding: var(--space-lg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-grey-light);
  }

  .mistake-card__header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .mistake-card__header h4 { font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .mistake-card__fix { font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-grey); margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* Plan */
  .plan-weeks { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-xl); margin-block: var(--space-xl); }

  .plan-week {
    background: var(--color-white);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-grey-light);
    border-radius: var(--border-radius-lg);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .plan-week__header {
    padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
    background: var(--color-ivory);
    border-left: 5px solid var(--color-orange);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
  }

  .plan-week__number {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--color-orange);
    display: block;
  }

  .plan-week__theme {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-dark);
    display: block;
  }

  .plan-week__sessions { padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg); }

  .plan-session {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    padding-block: var(--space-sm);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-grey-light);
    align-items: flex-start;
  }
  .plan-session:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  .plan-session__label {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-grey);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding-top: 2px;
  }

  .plan-session__detail {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-grey-dark);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .plan-week__tip {
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-grey);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-grey-light);
    background: var(--color-ivory);
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* Disclaimer */
  .disclaimer-box {
    background: #fff8f6;
    border: 2px solid rgba(255,107,53,0.3);
    border-radius: var(--border-radius-lg);
    padding: var(--space-xl);
  }
  .disclaimer-box p, .disclaimer-box li {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--color-grey-dark);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .disclaimer-box p { margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }
  .disclaimer-box ul { margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }

  /* Related links */
  .related-link-card {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: var(--space-lg);
    background: var(--color-ivory);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-grey-light);
    border-radius: var(--border-radius);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--color-dark);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    transition: border-color var(--transition), background-color var(--transition);
  }
  .related-link-card:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-orange);
    background-color: rgba(255,107,53,0.04);
    color: var(--color-orange);
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .mistakes-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .plan-session { flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-xs); }
    .plan-session__label { min-width: auto; }
  }
</style>
